::GEO_U06_L05_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L05_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.14
Question:
Which formula gives the volume of a rectangular prism?
{
=A. V = lwh#A rectangular prism volume is length times width times height.
~B. V = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh#This is surface area.
~C. V = 1/3Bh#This is for a pyramid or cone.
~D. V = pir?h#This is cylinder volume.
}


::GEO_U06_L05_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L05_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.14
Question:
A pyramid has base area 30 and height 9. What is its volume?
{
~A. 270 cubic units#This omits the one-third factor.
=B. 90 cubic units#Pyramid volume is 1/3Bh, so 1/3(30)(9)=90.
~C. 39 cubic units#This adds base area and height.
~D. 810 cubic units#This multiplies by 3 instead of dividing by 3.
}


::GEO_U06_L05_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L05_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.14
Question:
Which formula gives the volume of a sphere?
{
~A. 4pir?#This is sphere surface area.
~B. pir?h#This is cylinder volume.
=C. 4/3pir?#Sphere volume is 4/3pir?.
~D. 1/3pir?h#This is cone volume.
}


::GEO_U06_L05_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L05_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.14
Question:
A cylinder has radius 5 and height 2. What is its volume?
{
~A. 10pi cubic units#This multiplies radius and height but does not square the radius.
~B. 20pi cubic units#This does not use r? correctly.
~C. 25pi cubic units#This finds base area only.
=D. 50pi cubic units#Cylinder volume is pir?h = pi(5)?(2)=50pi.
}


::GEO_U06_L05_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L05_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.14
Question:
Which statement about volume units is correct?
{
=A. Volume uses cubic units because it measures three-dimensional space#Volume measures length, width, and height effects.
~B. Volume uses square units because solids have surfaces#Square units describe surface area, not volume.
~C. Volume uses degrees because solids have angles#Degrees measure angles.
~D. Volume has no units#A real measurement needs appropriate units.
}


